In the `resolveType` method, there are multiple checks for unresolved types. Consider using early returns to simplify the logic and improve readability:
```java
@Override
protected TypeResolution resolveType() {
    if (childrenResolved() == false) {
        return new TypeResolution("Unresolved children");
    }
    TypeResolution resolution = isString(str, sourceText(), FIRST);
    if (resolution.unresolved()) {
        return resolution;
    }
    resolution = isString(substr, sourceText(), SECOND);
    if (resolution.unresolved()) {
        return resolution;
    }
    return start == null ? TypeResolution.TYPE_RESOLVED : isInteger(start, sourceText(), THIRD);
}
```

In the `eval` method for `BytesRefBlock`, there is a repeated pattern of checking if a block is null and appending null to the result builder. This can be refactored into a separate method to reduce code duplication and improve readability. For example:
```java
private void handleNullValue(IntBlock.Builder result, int position) {
    if (strBlock.isNull(position) || strBlock.getValueCount(position) != 1 ||
        substrBlock.isNull(position) || substrBlock.getValueCount(position) != 1 ||
        startBlock.isNull(position) || startBlock.getValueCount(position) != 1) {
        warnings.registerException(new IllegalArgumentException("single-value function encountered multi-value"));
        result.appendNull();
    }
```
This method can be called at the beginning of the loop to handle null values and reduce redundancy.

